#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167904} :activity/id #uuid "70c56636-a16b-5974-b39b-c312a1a1e556" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168009 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/b0/b083c5707c4e9aed3141fe638c55a1ed2dc3ad6569f122bef307550c94fe6a8b.svg)\nwhere the eigenvalues of a ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are\n![](images/ee/eee119e3e35f381a1d66d9e1e75b27d89e68d5f72307f4e792887d5d7e9d0099.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/37/37030865839993f96fced21145844e8fca9c6d44e7922c623fe8e0bef98ef154.svg) where ![](images/0d/0d26960845a9f13b07c87e80faa88162c1dda64123f13567958d00aaa0a4dc94.svg) be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ![](images/fa/faa0f885195eacb91222dc9ccc18cf7ab891a17bd787359d13bf4e72bf09d703.svg)\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n![](images/c4/c44ac9f2de14beffd3411373047316c888a93815d2f90d338f500ca329f66355.svg)\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n![](images/e9/e90f20affdff00fd5fb24e5b6f75c31d42a0e5b2ad52b38b2d4456ee6555bfc8.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n![](images/62/6224eb38da8f189696cbda057a5568d71310d64574d36e80af6e1a11ceed6d6e.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/42/42bbe4d12055825e9c24b2dfc1a6ca306cb2675c13d039fbdb67e2170788c612.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca0400387ee619b6e2ddc7028859cf5902aecb715c38ec71e43b0f9056533cb6.svg) are bounded, so the factor ![](images/b0/b051bd1eb312485960edb54d28ffe85da63acab1c5f20b59466d01f55cba034c.svg) controls the long-term behavior:\n\n- If ![](images/0a/0a23a6577131711d405eb650b1d9d8429ca07d07bb9759644c407121643dcf5e.svg) then ![](images/9a/9a15a0d668c2a7ab9457f6a4c605858e03ddbb3ff93f3667d0deb0def1e206b1.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) So, trajectories are *unbounded*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward infinity as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) In particular, the trajectory is unbounded for ![](images/bb/bb0d93faa0ccc44575fe83f9f7a644895ced86c18e9908a8e0d6dc6a36378d7d.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/28/28e6012b2b10c0a7f40681ab8a73666a12afd6e976c59f536ed30d1defca5f64.svg) then ![](images/2b/2b74153553b7dab9ad3a92e31ddd3b09ba44e4ba0e451229e924f227087fb58b.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) So, trajectories *approach the origin*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) In particular, the trajectory is bounded for ![](images/23/23f355a7475101ff2d8f72842b5c291fcddc7b934d9e6a05c63b71c50443a334.svg) although it is unbounded if ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) is allowed to approach ![](images/8e/8ef8c99c128deb7895aefe6d1a662021e675d3609017391dd57526f775a30207.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/ba/ba33162d712db6f36bcf09e725d626c399881e98edb06cdc4ddfd17532b06701.svg) then ![](images/88/88318ea5b5d7fde77eace946e939b02b531330ff006ea20c887ea2f654acbdf9.svg) for all ![](images/a9/a90dc11635a0cfd7e959687183800180490f43955de346f70950a5720cd92df4.svg) So, trajectories are *bounded*, and we have an *ellipse*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a925aab-4f30-57a3-a3a2-6c3e21b5b318" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b0e4834-5b7b-5ae6-a7c1-b1def5543b1d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/d1/d13abb97b171049a17544fcb312b71d2da3d9294788c1aebe7aa969e99b7819c.svg)\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3d4213d-a0e4-5bda-9e29-8eca4801de63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14483 :tutorial/title "Systems With Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "89ecb234-3886-5849-970a-abafcfaa1df5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d"} :step/id #uuid "94fe1e57-9c9a-561e-bfd5-2ae1e6a4369a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a"} :step/id #uuid "6468cc1a-a1a6-502b-82f1-16821ba5d8c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168025 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n![](images/65/6597a81f818926afff9056180de0e12357ef7631be093aba32626a7461079c18.svg)\nwhere the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2}=a\\pm bi,\n\\qquad b\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- The type of the curve is determined by the parameter $a{:}$\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have bounded closed curves (ellipses/circles).\n\n- If $a \\neq 0,$ we have spirals.\n\n- The stability is determined by the sign of $a{:}$\n\n- If $a>0,$ we have an **unstable spiral** (source).\n\n- If $a<0,$ we have a **stable spiral** (sink).\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have a **center**.\n\n**Note:** The center is a *stable* equilibrium but not *asymptotically stable* since trajectories do not converge to it.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c36d256f-edd3-5079-9f72-da6e3a8b4f91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14575 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "6209ec28-abe9-501f-8925-5c8a06aee7df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3"} :step/id #uuid "4d18ae8d-2ebf-569f-bebb-2a856d39ef9d"}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167012} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524083} :activity/id #uuid "f52aa899-b1e0-52c3-865d-5560a2dc7dfa" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523972 :question/id #uuid "78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853"} :step/id #uuid "3d934a50-1cd4-5216-bb35-6060f0b5e4e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524032 :question/id #uuid "ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd"} :step/id #uuid "9fb9d22f-4511-506a-bc68-866d4beb9b4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167242 :question/id #uuid "84ee34e5-631b-5bc6-a73e-d75bd75ac74d"} :step/id #uuid "a015cce1-74cb-5e4f-9268-b88d722a4a5f"}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523248} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549092} :activity/id #uuid "d990f3a7-db2b-51f0-8db9-f30c83207610" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385185 :question/id #uuid "7f5d02c9-9292-553c-8b42-111175736740"} :step/id #uuid "17236c24-ba39-5f16-a290-16a88cca22fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167229 :question/id #uuid "0d2fdbc8-ed30-59a9-869b-569e3f094dfe"} :step/id #uuid "42f0ca7b-cd86-541c-96a8-c8c341a14559" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167242 :question/id #uuid "84ee34e5-631b-5bc6-a73e-d75bd75ac74d"} :step/id #uuid "ad94239e-f4c8-525d-8841-959377d0f69a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549082}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548352}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166550 :question/id #uuid "8337af7a-73e8-5ae2-9bed-b03f193b2950" :question/math-academy-id "e-21657" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/2b/2bc77eafe7c4362a1020c1f547a19ef0e72c309ebb82d1480e2ffec5bdfd8b38.svg)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/b0/b0df9855fa710d30217ffbe12135de27a9aa50e55b639418829b649ee6eb420c.svg)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y &=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/3c/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "931c80f3-d825-589f-b2ef-dcfd7dda5e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cos (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164403} {:answer/id #uuid "9d745451-32e5-56f5-873e-5d44bd359783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164404} {:answer/id #uuid "c7476285-a61e-526b-9239-dbfa7e20ad68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164405} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7038c7-076d-5f7d-8904-412d4024c10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164406}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6d633e3b-d76d-5046-9677-2e813d21d36a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b19b13-8e85-5cca-9303-b0250b36e640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164408} {:answer/id #uuid "d626414d-c8aa-5082-ac3a-5c14e336a24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164409} {:answer/id #uuid "57aff925-7607-5450-aedb-4ec3835384c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164410}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3f52f1a0-26bd-5086-a61e-12a04c44607c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164407}] :question/id #uuid "191930ff-089c-512d-9d8e-92738de0a404" :question/math-academy-id "q-332878" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 9x(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = ± 6\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± 2\\textrm{i}, 3]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sin (6t) \\\\ 3\\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (6t) \\\\ 3\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$3\\sin (6t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874415b0-df8a-5d98-aefd-3d33311031b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164412} {:answer/id #uuid "17b9fe3b-f204-50db-96bd-b79d4707a3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164413} {:answer/id #uuid "1e88e169-0a8b-5961-858b-5d8acd8994c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}\\cos (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164414} {:answer/id #uuid "402c709e-102d-5b25-ab1b-e412508e9e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}\\sin (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164415}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f34cf733-524f-5ca5-9835-2020e16946c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae0cf8e-0d2f-510d-a36a-f1cca1c2ed1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164417} {:answer/id #uuid "a132bcb6-734a-5ea8-9a3b-b73dc5dacf8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164418} {:answer/id #uuid "2756743a-d047-51ec-b58c-9c7f1fd4dfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}\\sin (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164419} {:answer/id #uuid "49d8987c-7f51-526e-bf43-cf96bae876b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}\\cos (3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164420}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d928a704-a7c0-5686-bdb0-d472cd60cdd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164416}] :question/id #uuid "b7c91956-938e-54e4-8d9f-be7b3c0b094d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332880" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - 3y(t); y^{′}(t) = 3x(t) + 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1, 2} = 2 ± 3\\textrm{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1, 2} = [± \\textrm{i}, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = c_{1}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nFor example, when $c_{1} = 1$ and $c_{2} = 0$, we obtain the following curve:\n$e^{2t}\\sin (3t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1f7d2b-7737-5d6f-b1d3-38ade4b48d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305275} {:answer/id #uuid "7e53c5be-789c-53ec-ab7b-33e604c99edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306359} {:answer/id #uuid "f9496859-5c78-5511-a473-7ba037c12c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307460} :answer-field/id #uuid "281925e0-c622-5b12-be72-f47b528ccb78" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5afa1989-0b81-51e3-a09c-e1924d66e52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "phase portrait" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306023} {:answer/id #uuid "f183aac4-afd0-5b24-b588-862e13e81693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value