 :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeb38c4-8164-50a5-83e3-05ef45c4cfbe" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427135}] :question/id #uuid "213c64ee-0522-5cdd-a84f-aee7cd552b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-333752" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-1\\pm 3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.54$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.54$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/30/30d1e296e7c83fa8925feb116c9035de14790e89ddad37ab44710feacadf13a8.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/05/054b319170c634494c703d9588419cb6e44414196c970ca0c39689bc62f90d97.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ y &={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.54$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.54$ | $0.59$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/30/30d1e296e7c83fa8925feb116c9035de14790e89ddad37ab44710feacadf13a8.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/05/054b319170c634494c703d9588419cb6e44414196c970ca0c39689bc62f90d97.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8b0a43-31f1-5fe6-9ec0-7118a4751741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427002} {:answer/id #uuid "89494851-9073-5ac1-be1d-05debbac8065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427039} {:answer/id #uuid "e791d48c-f3d9-5ce4-bf63-a75f6856114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427087} {:answer/id #uuid "f787aa9