olution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ (circle) that is traversed $\\text{clockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78cd39aa-7c90-5687-8921-043f0fa60b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "clockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306301} {:answer/id #uuid "c000e321-1f93-5d5b-8be0-0582d02d5083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306942} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0c5c9c-4b15-55f5-af61-95dd51b0b571" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75921d4a-63e5-5aa7-9cd5-c509cdb8734a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>0" :d