on of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1965cc4-138a-59cb-b873-02c363d95405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307091} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d1635c-7655-57fd-8bb9-9bec52814f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307306} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c1b14a-0e24-5eef-b8ce-dad624ac68c0" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342a3cb7-ff50-58ef-8f5c-9bf641ce5b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305646} {:a