s given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd47a1a-8da1-50b6-a767-dc9eb92e7d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305433} {:answer/id #uuid "5c76fbdb-fcb5-5542-8156-6f4e195f6572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306039} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2c4e02-b749-576b-a0a9-d427ae3b0b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305433} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f06eae-5283-536e-a06c-617d152da270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6a