  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd91dd1-c90f-56c1-8a22-a4fb47fa5f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523310} {:answer/id #uuid "e2128144-a4af-5256-b36a-5181f7ed5ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523310} :answer-field/id #uuid "083b0ba0-7609-5ec6-a938-3390e2910826" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f84dc65-c809-58fe-9248-5825204dea7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523415} {:answer/id #uuid "52d8604d-3b08-5dfc-b1df-7bfe0e66fe43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523415} :answer-field/id #uuid "43cb95e9-51cf-599a-bd5e-a3c29f1f9c2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c949f2-67bb-5cc5-ab89-4f36613e0369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "clockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523367} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6e12f4-9d18-5592-b8ff-e4e7d21e2354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "clockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523367} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ed1170-20ac-57d4-8bc2-7e1c095e6a0c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5af3527-a165-5d7d-83fe-e90f66fd95dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523669} {:answer/id #uuid "ce65b786-439c-5486-ad9e-d2dd2ab1cd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523686} {:answer/id #uuid "debc4a56-1da5-5922-a8fd-52a2d630e6b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523686} :answer-field/id #uuid "f17043e1-9f96-5f60-95a4-3f465e8429cb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523911}] :question/id #uuid "ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-334268" :question/problem "![](images/97/97291bd2f6b0a958a6651a87076a8ea313bec31cd6004840e0d81247dc364bf8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{clockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166552 :question/id #uuid "d7b40689-7b7e-546e-9b66-30e6a95f3ee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21659" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d09c368-867e-5295-888f-2d5522e93320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305264} {:answer/id #uuid "5beb30d1-23c2-5075-8b1d-e091046c0f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306034} {:answer/id #uuid "756d2617-b102-56a0-a522-8dec631437dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305264} :answer-field/id #uuid "d914bbc6-a082-50f7-9cda-a921caccdc7a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d876dd6-796c-5def-b786-b647e283b215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305427} {:answer/id #uuid "63a2a224-f9e6-5b06-a51f-fab909a03aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} {:answer/id #uuid "9288b3fd-6217-5dc0-a6cc-d0617247a0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306540} {:answer/id #uuid "a37e88b2-535b-56bf-a159-1c96e37285da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306691} {:answer/id #uuid "b15a28f9-090d-55da-8b02-5c72519ebdaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306817} {:answer/id #uuid "c00d3090-adac-5dc6-9f55-3e991a3b631d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2c442a-e93e-5d94-8ad6-003d643e840d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308316}] :question/id #uuid "09a5051d-6218-52fc-9de8-8eb98b5a1f5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332952" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af7a9dc-4c2f-5d69-b8b9-e695b84c2f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305400} {:answer/id #uuid "7878f51f-d804-58c5-bbb2-126626f606e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306295} {:answer/id #uuid "8d65aa67-e049-5ab2-be2c-e47dc05b3ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306491} {:answer/id #uuid "b5704042-e859-5471-b4d8-3792fd92117a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306848} {:answer/id #uuid "c999002e-f3c1-54e9-93d6-6892d5b98533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307028} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bfd246-14eb-5fe0-9e8d-b2270ae2da03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307028} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c363d9-b742-572e-abef-45e29ff56aa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a34d424-e407-558f-bd81-fc4783e40494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305869} {:answer/id #uuid "b4934526-0b23-59bd-ab5c-0e68570ec744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306845} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cadb25-011c-5ce5-9daf-8e2c73548bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306867} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cc31f2-55a8-51f8-a762-087d512fc575" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308232}] :question/id #uuid "dd7f6a8a-46ea-55af-8487-3a48b0b2a59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334307" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fefd856-04bf-577c-84fb-f1491b46729e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305463} {:answer/id #uuid "23bc3010-