torname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=2>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a177b1d-8f4d-5ce4-96d5-0641118ffbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427001} {:answer/id #uuid "44d3d3bc-85e9-5891-a136-208e1a7ae81c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427009} {:answer/id #uuid "59791c19-fe10-5a49-8c0d-1483606ff223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427016} {:answer/id #uuid "61bacac7-6dd5-50c2-956e-a48c2f0f54d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427020} {:answer/id #uuid "6a53884e-4438-5bc3-ba6b-7c3120bf76ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427027} {:answer/id #uuid "fd467763-82ff-5759-bf17-4bb9e315a854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427009} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d312be2-8e22-53a7-b0ac-f747e3a9aeda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f18f9e5-125e-52fa-a4bf-f9023b9d06e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427013} {:answer/id #uuid "8c413faf-a70e-5b39-b44f-b5ca5bb38ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427041} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c8c4d4-3353-5b89-8ca9-42cba1be9033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427092} :answer-field/id #uuid "73156d6c-fd43-56c5-bed5-47e0532e4a14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427120}] :question/id #uuid "c1fcfe84-fe11-5391-abf0-f5881c1a0ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334305" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=8x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =5\\pm 8\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb8140c-7d21-5826-b8c6-8681a1f60f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533282} {:answer/id #uuid "c11c5670-2897-5382-a387-a92972ba5b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533606} {:answer/id #uuid "cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533630} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecaf0db-258e-5d47-8f5d-62e1aab6739b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d50709-aa76-582b-9410-59b11f93d7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533196} {:answer/id #uuid "227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533223} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf21272-b21f-5d98-9834-5ad7916a9c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533324} {:answer/id #uuid "620c9ad3-6c3f-5474-acac-ef780aed0dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533364} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd13b7f-f0ef-539c-8da4-07661c578421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533634} {:answer/id #uuid "d292b233-658f-5ac4-a192-6e5e28d2210a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533223} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf45c67d-750d-505d-989a-2337e3e5243c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533918}] :question/id #uuid "cbeb2c34-b611-5622-babc-25dc1311116c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334309" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-3<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3241 :topic/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093063} :activity/id #uuid "dc617afc-4448-553a-86e9-df31a8dee17e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093436 :tutorial/content "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose we know that some vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\mathbf w$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ where the vector $\\mathbf w$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe mapping $\\mathbf T(\\mathbf v) = \\mathbf w$ is shown schematically in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b5/b53ed555a52830fd0dcc0ba150c618fd8b527a0261c8e3caf09378c47dc6ffae.png)\n\nHow can we find the original vector $\\mathbf v?$\n\nTo find the vector $\\mathbf v,$ we compute the inverse of the matrix $T$ and then use it to reverse the action of $\\mathbf T.$ Let's see how this works. \n\nFrom the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we pre-multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation tells that to find the vector $\\mathbf v,$ we need to pre-multiply the vector $\\mathbf w$ by the inverse of $T.$\n\nSo, we now calculate the inverse of $T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{20-16}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the original vector $\\mathbf v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that this is the correct answer by computing $T\\cdot \\mathbf v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe summarize the process we just completed in the following diagram.\n\n![](images/69/6991d3f7be5f29f51512ed927189ea10cbbcdb9dc6a2ce00573759201fb684a6.png)\n\nIn general, if $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that represents a linear transformation, then $T^{-1}$ is the inverse linear transformation and, if it exists, it reverses the action of $T.$ So for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ on the plane, we have\n\n$$\nT^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot \\mathbf{v} = I_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "430eb3b0-4669-5c55-a39d-eed3fd6cb13f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ccaac050-b186-57be-b44f-9a0b20e3cc93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388"} :step/id #uuid "c041a7fc-585b-50bc-a484-d52801542d68" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c"} :step/id #uuid "25ac9ce0-4bad-55a3-ba7d-6e41e7741c24"}] :activity/title "Inverting Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092462}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049213 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092070 :question/id #uuid "90308076-a0ef-5e6a-900d-2ef4c8107907" :question/math-academy-id "e-5466" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n![](images/37/371521f0acd44811e109c10460e0cc99eb41d256f37f209dcefc003fd78ea70e.png)\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n![](images/c9/c95b308a6f55de3acc8a2bf446b38ac10500dd506795898b0892952147d5c9e7.png)\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98e9eb4f-4a61-50c0-8ee7-ed9290595c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091049} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd20ebb-67ee-5259-9f15-014b8e579c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091050} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba0a921-934f-568c-bdad-43e4e4d59235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091051} {:answer/id #uuid "4e69912f-d74a-5932-a652-ca385c00de93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091052} {:answer/id #uuid "6484842d-5fd6-5baf-92ff-f9b375f1c5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091053}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3ff692-b865-5eaf-99a2-19206b648c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091048}] :question/id #uuid "a530e065-25bc-55a5-a4e2-57fb7d3dcdd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70645" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is the original vector $v$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9899e814-7f31-52e1-8dfd-9e34535d37b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091055} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6bed2a-36c3-5e1c-9c79-b8b932cf34c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091056} {:answer/id #uuid "cd87dca3-d530-564b-ac35-9831a8294fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091057} {:answer/id #uuid "095dc30d-d733-5d4d-9c5d-8a9fee22d39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091058} {:answer/id #uuid "42970d8b-4d36-58ba-9d17-11f9e50697df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091059}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6ef63f49-716c-5070-b94c-7b8bdacecbd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091054}] :question/id #uuid "e0a08109-3dd0-53cf-87f7-c396be497cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-70646" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is the original vector $v$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d97df79-6803-5a18-a3bc-61908206f4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250804} {:answer/id #uuid "207aefcd-f554-5cbd-8870-3636e6a632bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250828} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc539f8-f7d0-5aa5-9a90-8d1394a0f50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250929} {:answer/id #uuid "8475ae5e-44e1-5e86-bd1f-d1e6f6b2ec8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251664} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd1433-8d78-5b22-a794-1ae4f68764b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c32ab28d-18db-5b14-9415-686e1bd875f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253143}] :question/id #uuid "c42e6ce8-2785-5191-9d67-38a51c8bea0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11972" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-(-8)}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f86fc83-c992-5f5a-afcb-93e7b57e70cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253809} {:answer/id #uuid "22528c8a-972f-5b87-897d-d3e5b5590cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253978} {:answer/id #uuid "39680fd4-fa9b-5228-bcd0-f9d831641111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254172} {:answer/id #uuid "997f3d9a-7113-5062-b036-b7dbe6ddffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255026} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d45517-4240-5252-81bf-1f5fcb8b594f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253978} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6da18cd-bfa4-5e9a-831d-eb1abb1267cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256228}] :question/id #uuid "d91202bf-09b3-51de-856a-c9de693cfec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-12271" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-(-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ca33630-8278-5592-9872-446a68f1e832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276616} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7fac0c-b29a-5ea2-8ae0-daa528b03a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276634} {:answer/id #uuid "9280d8d4-bf1b-5f69-a9dd-3b969ff6c675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276803} {:answer/id #uuid "968d834b-34c7-5bda-8fd1-8e5febf1c3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276831} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa7b754-1de0-5e60-b0f9-ce840ca24195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276616} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7711751-7a59-5c07-9f6d-59e3263e92e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278015}] :question/id #uuid "e3579af1-4491-5958-8694-2b4f12dcd55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159975" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dcacd1-c1ee-5404-9b42-508772f45ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291606} :answer-field/id #uuid "503ce861-b007-5067-b683-b2b46009e959" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b338926-98f9-5496-b121-f1db573523e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290951} :answer-field/id #uuid "e09e99f3-6fd3-57c8-af4c-94ebc7eb19fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292435}] :question/id #uuid "7837cc3d-c51c-5719-b51b-06bfee4699cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-276283" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{20-16}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82887a1-f1f7-5270-b9bc-29a653771ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298352} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43e1e03-e1b6-5cb8-9d61-776b22be328f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a724dfd4-c8fd-590a-8d7b-426c350292ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298283} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e23b21-a929-5251-94d9-77a7d911a684" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299201}] :question/id #uuid "b6998f43-65a5-5cc0-add9-e811dcb25991" :question/math-academy-id "q-317429" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{12+10}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{19}{22}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbae8988-fd7f-5342-836f-ee693f27ec71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298626} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d326f5-2af3-55af-a796-5d88b5d42d08" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2707fced-6449-57a7-8600-a42e6e400d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee18f046-2dca-5120-8448-cee1a53e33ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299440}] :question/id #uuid "d6acdc38-da00-500e-be7e-e54f4a05aa5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-317431" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+15}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ \\frac{25}{17}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22baafbe-5e5e-5bc8-9b6c-03984c49a32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297702} :answer-field/id #uuid "2170e52b-2fd6-5db6-a995-77869f2e70f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298748